skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: Explain how you run the kdevkit dev loop on one coherent slice of implementation work: the gates and their order, what happens on a failing gate, who runs code review, and how you handle a mid-dev change prompted by review feedback.
expect: A correct answer covers the dev loop as an ordered gate sequence and the reasoning around it. (1) Gate order: Quality (format, lint, type-check — deterministic only) → Test → Code Review → Push, in that order; tests must be green before the reviewer sees the diff. (2) Failing gate: on failure the agent diagnoses and fixes within a retry budget, re-running earlier gates; a sub-threshold code review sends the work back into the loop — re-running the earlier gates its fixes invalidate (the Quality Gate when the fixes were substantial, per the skill; a narrower re-run is correct for a small fix), and by default (hard-stop) a still-failing gate refuses the push rather than shipping. (3) Code review is run by a separate/fresh-context reviewer — not the implementing agent — seeing project.md and the diff but not the feature spec. (4) A reactive mid-dev change (from review feedback or a verify finding) that moves, renames, or re-scopes a component or alters a contract triggers a re-pin: the agent re-checks owner / altitude / reuse / symmetry against project.md before writing the fix, rather than patching at the point of failure. Wrong answers: putting Code Review before Test, or inside Quality; letting the implementing agent review its own diff; feeding the reviewer the feature spec by default; skipping the re-pin and patching symptoms; describing a self-scored review step instead of a separate reviewer. Judge coverage of the gates, the failure path, who reviews, and the reactive-change rule. An answer that is MORE precise than this narrative — naming a conditional where this says always, or a retry budget this omits — is correct, not wrong; verify against the skill before failing such a claim.
